\p{A field assigns a coefficient to each vertex or edge. Along an
oriented edge, the incidence map subtracts the source value from the
head value. Endpoint pullbacks express this comparison between distinct
field spaces. Composition supplies
an operator product on a common field module; comparing it with pointwise
field multiplication gives a discrete product rule. The
[alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) makes this rule and the failure of
operator commutativity concrete.}

\p{The coefficient ring is commutative and unital; the operator
product need not commute. Positive counting pairings and weights enter
in the [real specialization](fdip-0002). Working over a ring first
separates identities of incidence, composition and elimination from
positivity and integration. The general adjoint construction assumes
perfect pairings, and elimination requires an invertible eliminated block;
these hypotheses do not follow from the coefficient ring alone.
Real positivity and measure are additional data for the later Gaussian
laws. Starting from a product has a
geometric-algebra precedent in
\citet{author-PDF pp. 5-6, Equations (1.1)-(1.6)}{hestenes1986unified};
Doran and Lasenby explain their delayed use of coordinates in
\citet{Preface, p. x}{doran2003geometric}. Here the product is composition
of specified maps, distinct from the Clifford geometric product.}

